"},{"symbol":"b","type":"integer frequency"},{"symbol":"c","type":"integer frequency"}],"objects":["a","b","c"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/11","record":{"id":"D012","symbol":"Nthird","type":"positive third-largest-frequency function","definition":"For nonzero integers a,b,c,d with a+b+c+d=0, let N1>=N2>=N3>=N4 be the decreasing rearrangement of |a|,|b|,|c|,|d|, resolving ties in any order. Nthird(a,b,c,d)=N3. Thus N3 is the second smallest of all four magnitudes including the dual output.","depends_on":[],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"a","type":"nonzero integer"},{"symbol":"b","type":"nonzero integer"},{"symbol":"c","type":"nonzero integer"},{"symbol":"d","type":"nonzero integer"}],"objects":["a","b","c","d"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/12","record":{"id":"D013","symbol":"R2","type":"family of trilinear Fourier operators initially on mean-zero trigonometric polynomials","definition":"For tau>0, real base time t_n and k!=0, hat R2_(t_n,tau)(f1,f2,f3)(k)=-(tau/(18*i*k))*sum_{a+b+c=k,a+b!=0}exp(-i*t_n*Phi)*eta_tau(alpha,beta)*hat f1(a)*hat f2(b)*hat f3(c), with zero coefficient at k=0; Phase gives alpha,beta,Phi. Write R2^n_tau for t_n=n*tau. Bounded extensions, when used, are proved below.","depends_on":["D002","D010","D011"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"tau","type":"positive real step"},{"symbol":"t_n","type":"real base time"},{"symbol":"k","type":"integer output"},{"symbol":"f1","type":"first polynomial input"},{"symbol":"f2","type":"second polynomial input"},{"symbol":"f3","type":"third polynomial input"},{"symbol":"a","type":"summed integer"},{"symbol":"b","type":"summed integer"},{"symbol":"c","type":"summed integer"}],"objects":["Fourier","eta","Phase","tau","t_n","k","f1","f2","f3","a","b","c"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/13","record":{"id":"D014","symbol":"pstar","type":"positive real exponent","definition":"pstar=23/14.","depends_on":[],"requires":[],"binders":[],"objects":[]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/14","record":{"id":"D015","symbol":"B","type":"time-dependent bilinear distribution-valued map","definition":"B_t(f,g)=S_(-t) partial_x[(S_t f)(S_t g)]. Initially use smooth inputs; the negative Sobolev extension to L^2 inputs is proved below.","depends_on":["D004","D007"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"t","type":"real time"},{"symbol":"f","type":"first input"},{"symbol":"g","type":"second input"}],"objects":["S","Dinv","t","f","g"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/15","record":{"id":"D016","symbol":"J","type":"time-dependent bilinear map","definition":"J_t(f,g)=S_(-t)P[(S_t Dinv f)(S_t Dinv g)].","depends_on":["D004","D006","D007"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"t","type":"real time"},{"symbol":"f","type":"first input"},{"symbol":"g","type":"second input"}],"objects":["S","P","Dinv","t","f","g"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/16","record":{"id":"D017","symbol":"Ctri","type":"interval-dependent trilinear integral","definition":"For I=[a,b], C_I(f,g,h)=integral_a^b S_(-t)P{P[(S_t f(t))(S_t g(t))]*(S_t Dinv h(t))}dt. Constant inputs are identified with constant time paths. At smooth inputs this is an ordinary integral, and the source estimates supply its continuous extensions.","depends_on":["D004","D006","D007"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"I","type":"interval"},{"symbol":"a","type":"left endpoint"},{"symbol":"b","type":"right endpoint"},{"symbol":"t","type":"integration variable"},{"symbol":"f","type":"first path"},{"symbol":"g","type":"second path"},{"symbol":"h","type":"third path"}],"objects":["S","P","Dinv","I","a","b","t","f","g","h"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/17","record":{"id":"D018","symbol":"X","type":"norm on time paths with weak derivative","definition":"For an interval I, ||f||_(X(I))=||f||_(L^infty(I;L^2))+||partial_t f||_(L^infty(I;H^(-pstar))). Use the continuous L^2 representative wherever supplied.","depends_on":["D003","D014"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"I","type":"time interval"},{"symbol":"f","type":"time path"}],"objects":["Sob","pstar","I","f"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/18","record":{"id":"D019","symbol":"Stage","type":"family of bilinear integrated quadratic stages","definition":"For a base time a>=0, step tau>0, t in [a,a+tau], and constant mean-zero inputs f,g, F_a(t;f,g)=(1/2)integral_a^t B_s(f,g)ds. Abbreviate F_a(t;f)=F_a(t;f,f). Its endpoint representation and extensions are proved below.","depends_on":["D015"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"a","type":"nonnegative base time"},{"symbol":"tau","type":"positive step"},{"symbol":"t","type":"stage time"},{"symbol":"s","type":"integration variable"},{"symbol":"f","type":"first input"},{"symbol":"g","type":"second input"}],"objects":["B","a","tau","t","s","f","g"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/19","record":{"id":"D020","symbol":"KdV","type":"distributional evolution equation and smooth-approximation solution convention","definition":"KdV is u_t+u_xxx=(1/2)partial_x(u^2), u(0)=u0, with real mean-zero data on Torus. An exact solution at regularity sigma>=0 means its unique real mean-zero C([0,T];H^sigma) distributional solution obtained as the limit of smooth-data solutions. This convention alone grants neither existence nor a bound; these are proved from the registered flow source. For an existing solution write its twisted path v(t)=S_(-t)u(t).","depends_on":["D001","D003","D004"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"u0","type":"initial datum"},{"symbol":"u","type":"solution path"},{"symbol":"sigma","type":"nonnegative order"}],"objects":["Torus","Sob","S","T","u0","u","sigma"]}},{"pointer":"spec.json#/definitions/20","record":{"id":"D021","symbol":"Interp","type":"stepwise interpolation and residual construction from a smooth trajectory","definition":"For tau>0, t_n=n*tau, and a finite smooth mean-zero trajectory f_n, on I_n=[t_n,t_(n+1)] put F_n(t)=F_(t_n)(t;f_n,f_n), z_n=f_n+F_n, C_n(t)=integral_(t_n)^t B_s(f_n,F_n(s))ds, Q_n(t)=(1/2)integral_(t_n)^t B_s(F_n(s),F_n(s))ds, W_n=Q_n(t_(n+1))+R2_(t_n,tau)(f_n,f_n,f_n), V(t)=f_n+F_n(t)+C_n(t)+Q_n(t)-((t-t_n)/tau)*W_n, and Y_n=V-z_n. Put rho_n(t)=-integral_(t_n)^t B_s(Y_n,z_n)ds-(1/2)integral_(t_n)^t B_s(Y_n,Y_n)ds-((t-t_n)/tau)*W_n. The residual path mathcalR is the concatenation of these increments: mathcalR(0)=0 and mathcalR(t)=sum_(j<n)rho_j(t_(j+1))+rho_n(t) on I_n. This construction does not assert endpoint compatibility; that is proved when f_n are the twisted Scheme trajectory.","depends_on":["D013","D015","D019"],"requires":[],"binders":[{"symbol":"tau","type":"positive step"},{"symbol":"L","type":"positive trajectory length"},{"symbol":"f_n","type":"finite smooth mean-zero sequence"},{"symbol":"n","type":"integer step index"},{"symbol":"t","type":"step time"},{"symbol":"s","type":"integration variable"},{"symbol":"j","type":"past step index"}],"objects":["R2","B","Stage","tau","L","f_n","n","t","s","j"]}}],"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
