{"id": "external-source-evidence.json", "pointer": "", "view": "value", "value": {"E001": {"id": "E001", "source_contract": {"author": "Anatoli V. Babin, Alexei A. Ilyin, Edriss S. Titi", "title": "On the regularization mechanism for the periodic Korteweg-de Vries equation", "version": "arXiv:0910.1389v2, 23 October 2010, 42-page PDF", "identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/0910.1389v2", "locator": "Theorem 4.2 bound clause and Theorem 4.3, pp.13-15; Theorem 5.1 pp.19-21; Theorems 6.3-6.5 pp.27-28; definitions (3.1)-(3.12), pp.5-6.", "notation_map": "Source U_t=U U_x+U_xxx, on the same 2*pi torus, real mean-zero coefficients and normalized homogeneous norms. Source twisted y_k=exp(i*t*k^3)U_k. No sign conversion is built into E001; N001 proves it.", "errata": "No correction or withdrawal identified in the accessed primary text. This is a check of the cited edition and available metadata, not a claim that all possible later errata have been excluded.", "source_kind": "research preprint", "origin": "problem.md:105-122,825-827", "retrieved_at": "2026-11-03T17:50:08.721171+00:00", "source_statement": "Complete selected source statements, with the auxiliary normal-form conclusion of Theorem 4.2 unused:\n\nTheorem 4.2. Let M0 be given and let s0 ≥ 0. Assume that v(m)(0) = v0 ∈ ˙H s0 and\nsuppose further that ∥v(m)(0)∥ ˙H 0 ≤ M0. Let T > 0 and let v(m)(t) be a solution of (4.1)\nover [0,T ]. Then v(m)(t) solves (4.10) and, uniformly in m, satisﬁes the estimate\n∥v(m)(t)∥ ˙H s0 ≤Ms0 for 0 ≤t ≤T, (4.13)\nwhe…2684 tokens truncated…ea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.json", "sha256": "451b6190c171afe99896ce594f2658559b89018ab864f5a486b3950778c87a04"}, {"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.pdf", "sha256": "e71452d13d7d54b902c6d79a08bbcd68edab795f0a59366fff14399b98192f9b"}, {"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.txt", "sha256": "0f444ea8653c6f0f1f46b8446362060aa1ffafb328148f88bab72154fc62e22d"}], "primary_evidence": [{"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.pdf", "sha256": "e71452d13d7d54b902c6d79a08bbcd68edab795f0a59366fff14399b98192f9b"}, {"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.txt", "sha256": "0f444ea8653c6f0f1f46b8446362060aa1ffafb328148f88bab72154fc62e22d"}], "statement": "In the source's unnormalized function norms, for alpha in [0,1], 0<=a<=b, b-a<=1, paths f_j in L^infty([a,b];H^alpha) with weak time derivatives in L^infty([a,b];H^(-23/14)), set X0_j=||f_j||_(L^infty L^2)+||partial_t f_j||_(L^infty H^(-23/14)). The operator C_[a,b] in Ctri satisfies ||C_[a,b](f1,f2,f3)||_2 <= C*(b-a)*product_j X0_j + C*||f_i||_(L^infty H^(-23/14))*product_{j!=i}||f_j||_(L^infty L^2), for each i=1,2,3. If the three inputs are independent of time, then ||C_[a,b](f1,f2,f3)||_2 <= C_alpha*(b-a)^alpha*product_j ||f_j||_(H^alpha). At b=a the integral is zero; the exponent-zero estimate means a uniform bound. Constants are independent of endpoints within the fixed interval-length bound and of Fourier support."}, "documents": [{"source": {"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.json", "sha256": "451b6190c171afe99896ce594f2658559b89018ab864f5a486b3950778c87a04"}, "primary": false, "readback": "source-451b6190c171afe99896ce594f2658559b89018ab864f5a486b3950778c87a04.txt", "readback_kind": "EXACT_UTF8_BYTES", "readback_sha256": "451b6190c171afe99896ce594f2658559b89018ab864f5a486b3950778c87a04"}, {"source": {"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.pdf", "sha256": "e71452d13d7d54b902c6d79a08bbcd68edab795f0a59366fff14399b98192f9b"}, "primary": true, "readback": "source-e71452d13d7d54b902c6d79a08bbcd68edab795f0a59366fff14399b98192f9b.txt", "readback_kind": "PDF_TEXT_EXTRACTION", "converter": "pypdf", "readback_sha256": "0f444ea8653c6f0f1f46b8446362060aa1ffafb328148f88bab72154fc62e22d"}, {"source": {"path": "external-sources/access-3b25f994bea441c5a4488c5300f59ff6.txt", "sha256": "0f444ea8653c6f0f1f46b8446362060aa1ffafb